WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:06.700
As you can see, not all data makes the jump correctly from Twinmotion to Unreal.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.630
Another example of this is in the form of the animated doors.

3
00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:20.410
They are animated in Twinmotion as you get close to them, but that functionality has disappeared with the transfer to UE5.

4
00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:30.809
The rotators are still in place acting as hinges, as are the hierarchies, but not the animation.

5
00:00:30.809 --> 00:00:36.009
The good news is that you can replicate the effect in UE5 with the help of a simple blueprint.

6
00:00:36.009 --> 00:00:44.359
Adjust your view outside the doors on the upper floor. Also set Play Mode to activate from the current camera location.

7
00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:57.840
In the Theater folder in the Content Browser, create a BP for Blueprint subfolder.

8
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:12.859
In it, create a new actor blueprint and name it DoorOpen. Double-click the blueprint to edit it.

9
00:01:12.859 --> 00:01:20.459
Select one of the doors and browse to the asset in the Content Browser.

10
00:01:25.269 --> 00:01:28.269
Add a new Static Mesh component to the blueprint.

11
00:01:28.269 --> 00:01:37.879
Because the door asset is selected in the Content Browser, it will be automatically added to the Blueprint viewport.

12
00:01:37.879 --> 00:01:46.500
Simplify the name of the Static Mesh component in the blueprint as Door.

13
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:53.609
Select the default scene root entry and add Child Actor.

14
00:01:53.609 --> 00:01:59.189
This will act as a non-renderable null object and will be used as a hinge for the door.

15
00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:01.689
In fact, name it Hinge.

16
00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:09.719
Adjust the position of the door so that its right side is aligned with the hinge at 0,0,0.

17
00:02:09.719 --> 00:02:15.139
Use a Snap value of 1cm for better control.

18
00:02:15.139 --> 00:02:22.050
Parent the door to the hinge.

19
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.099
Next, you need a trigger box.

20
00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:30.349
Select the default scene root again and add a Box Collision component.

21
00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:36.379
Name it Trigger.

22
00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:43.620
Adjust its position and Extents to match the door.

23
00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:52.180
And also make sure it extends enough in the Y direction to allow a fair distance to trigger it as you step in.

24
00:02:52.180 --> 00:03:00.180
In the Event Graph tab, delete all existing nodes, you won't need them.

25
00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.659
Select the Trigger component and go lower in the Details panel.

26
00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:17.479
Click the On Component Begin Overlap and On Component End Overlap events.

27
00:03:17.479 --> 00:03:23.840
This will add nodes where you can define what happens when you step in or out of the trigger volume.

28
00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:33.120
Drag the Hinge component into the working area, and add Set Relative Rotation to it with a 90 degree Z rotation angle.

29
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:41.949
Connect the action to the Begin Overlap event.

30
00:03:41.949 --> 00:03:46.430
This will set the door to rotate 90 degrees when you step into the volume.

31
00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:56.240
Compile and save, and then drag an instance of the blueprint to the scene to test the results.

32
00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:01.669
Test the scene in Play mode.

33
00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:10.710
At this stage, the door opens with a snap and remains open.

34
00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:23.089
You could expand on the blueprint by duplicating nodes and resetting the rotation angle to 0 as you end the overlap.

35
00:04:23.089 --> 00:04:29.449
To duplicate nodes, select them and then press Ctrl-D.

36
00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:37.279
Compile and save.

37
00:04:37.279 --> 00:04:49.240
The effect works but the snapping is bothersome.

38
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.240
You need a smoother rotation.

39
00:04:51.240 --> 00:05:00.810
To create a smoother rotation, add a Timeline node.

40
00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:15.180
Rewire the connections to the door on Begin Overlap, and double-click the Timeline node to edit it.

41
00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:19.250
Add a float track named Door Opening.

42
00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:24.250
Here we'll define an animation curve to smoothly open the door between two angle values.

43
00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:28.920
With a right-click, add two keyframes to the curve.

44
00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:36.060
Select them both and set them to Auto Tangents.

45
00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:40.060
This will add ease-in, ease-out effects to the door opening and closing.

46
00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:45.480
Set the first key at a time position of 0 with a value of 0.

47
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:49.480
This means that the door is closed before you get into the trigger volume.

48
00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:58.699
Set the second key to a time position of 2, as in 2 seconds, and a value of 110.

49
00:05:58.699 --> 00:06:03.699
This essentially means that it'll take 2 seconds for the door to rotate 110 degrees.

50
00:06:03.699 --> 00:06:11.259
Use the Zoom buttons on the toolbar to view the extents of the curve.

51
00:06:11.259 --> 00:06:17.759
Go back to the event graph when done and note the new door opening pin representing the new timeline curve.

52
00:06:17.759 --> 00:06:25.120
Right-click the new rotation track in the Set Relative Rotation event and split the structure pin.

53
00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.319
This separates the Vector3 XYZ rotation and separate float tracks.

54
00:06:30.319 --> 00:06:35.139
Wire the door opening curve to the Z Relative Rotation of the hinge.

55
00:06:35.639 --> 00:06:45.480
To reverse the action of the door, you only need to wire the On Component End overlap node to the reverse pin of the timeline.

56
00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:49.889
You can delete any excess nodes at this time.

57
00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:59.329
Compile and save the blueprint, then test your work.

58
00:06:59.329 --> 00:07:16.889
The door should be behaving much better now.

59
00:07:16.889 --> 00:07:19.389
Delete the test instance when done.

60
00:07:19.389 --> 00:07:22.889
Next, you will replace the Twinmotion doors with the blueprint.

61
00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:27.089
Start with the upper door on the right as viewed from the bridge.

62
00:07:27.089 --> 00:07:32.660
It is actually made of two actors, a mesh door and a non-renderable rotator.

63
00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:40.300
You won't need the door at all as the blueprint you have defined already contains a duplicate, so go ahead and delete it.

64
00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:45.410
Select the remaining rotator actor.

65
00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:49.699
Make sure the blueprint is also selected in the Content Browser.

66
00:07:49.699 --> 00:07:55.240
Right-click the selected actor in the Outliner and replace it with the blueprint.

67
00:07:55.240 --> 00:08:00.839
Test your work.

68
00:08:00.839 --> 00:08:09.629
Note that the door is swinging to the incorrect side.

69
00:08:09.629 --> 00:08:18.649
Arguably the easiest way to fix this is by setting its Y scale to minus 1.

70
00:08:18.649 --> 00:08:34.409
This will reverse the swing.

71
00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:46.600
Repeat the procedure with the left door.

72
00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.600
This one will swing correctly and does not require Y scale adjustments.

73
00:08:50.600 --> 00:09:08.600
Repeat the procedure for the doors at the ground level.

74
00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:14.100
These will require slight adjustments to their X scale as well, as they are a little wider.

75
00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.100
A value of 1.35 should work well.

76
00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:34.899
You can now use the doors to get in and out of the building.

77
00:09:34.899 --> 00:09:36.899
Save your work before moving on.
